@* One island to choose from in the setup form: its picture, its name, and whatever the host
   hangs underneath it.

   A published layout and a shape from the player's own library are picked from the same tile
   on purpose — choosing an island should be choosing between pictures, whoever arranged them.
   IslandSetupDiagram draws either: a published layout from its shipped file, a saved shape
   from the geometry it was kept with.

   The footer and the corner action are siblings of the select button rather than inside it: a
   button may not contain a button, and what goes there — the record pill, and for the player's
   own shapes a bin — are real controls.

   Which island it shows, and everything read off that, is IslandTileBase — shared with the tile
   the boards index and the statistics page list islands with. *@
@inherits IslandTileBase

@* Both states are TileChrome's, shared with the board strip's tiles on the same screen — the two
   say "this is the one" and "this could be" identically, and each had spelled both out. *@
<div class="@(Selected ? TileChrome.Selected : TileChrome.Resting)
            relative flex flex-col rounded-xl border transition-all">
    <button type="button" @onclick="OnSelect" title="@Title"
            class="w-full flex-1 rounded-t-xl p-2 pb-1 text-center focus-ring">
        <div class="flex h-12 items-center justify-center">
            <IslandSetupDiagram Setup="Setup" Geometry="@Saved?.LayoutJson" BoardCount="BoardCount"
                                Label="@Name"/>
        </div>
        @* .tile-title — the name under a picture, as the index tile and the statistics page's
           layout row set it; this was the one island tile a pixel smaller than the others. *@
        <div class="tile-title mt-1 truncate">@Name</div>
    </button>

    @* On the corner rather than inside it, as the adversary chips on the setup scene do — the
       tile is only a picture wide and anything laid over the middle of it hides the island. *@
    @if (Action is not null)
    {
        <div class="absolute -right-1 -top-1">@Action</div>
    }

    @if (Footer is not null)
    {
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-1 pb-2">@Footer</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether this is the island currently set up.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Selected { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnSelect { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What is worth reporting about this island — its record pill.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Footer { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A control that acts on the island itself rather than describing it — the bin on a saved
    /// shape. Pinned to the tile's corner instead of standing beside the footer, where a wide
    /// pill ("Never played") pushes it onto a line of its own and the tile grows a row taller
    /// than the ones next to it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Action { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the tile can't fit: the board count, and when a saved shape was kept.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// IsSaved rather than a <c>Saved is null</c> test of its own: the base settles which of the
    /// two islands a tile is showing (a Setup wins), so asking the question a second way here is
    /// how a tile ends up drawing a published layout while its tooltip dates the saved one.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Title =>
        IsSaved
            ? $"{Name} — {Boards}, kept {SetupInsights.Ago(Saved!.UpdatedAt)}"
            : $"{Name} — {Boards}";
}
